course: dictionary
lesson: 11692
title: Attendance: being there
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Attendance means being present at a place or event, like school. When you are there, you are in attendance. «Good attendance means coming to school every day.» So good attendance means you are rarely absent.
explain: Attendance can also mean a number: how many people came. «The attendance at the concert was 300 people.» Here the word counts the people, so it is about how many came, not about one person being there.
explain: Look for phrases too. Schools take attendance, keep an attendance record, and some pupils have full attendance. The words around attendance tell you which meaning is needed.
fact: Attendance means being present at a place or event.
fact: The attendance is the number of people who came to an event.
fact: «In attendance» means being there, especially to help.
fact: Schools take attendance every day, and it is written in a record.
fact: In maths, attendance can be shown as a percentage, such as 90 per cent.
exercise: «Good attendance means coming to school every day.» What does attendance mean here? | Being late for school | *Being present at school | Being ill at home | Being in a different class
exercise: «The attendance at the concert was 300 people.» What does attendance mean here? | *The number of people who came | The name of the singer | The loudness of the music | The time the concert began
exercise: «The head teacher was in attendance at the prize-giving.» What does in attendance mean? | Late for the event | Missing from the event | *Being there, especially to help | Sitting outside the hall
test: Mr Hall says, «Please take attendance.» What must he do? | Write a story | *Record who is present | Give out the lunch | Count the books
test: «Full attendance» on a school report means what? | Some pupils came on some days | Everybody was late | *Every pupil came every day | The school was full of people
exam: In a class of 30 pupils, 27 were present. What was the attendance as a percentage? | 27 per cent | 30 per cent | *90 per cent | 3 per cent
